  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nAs a low level jet (LLJ) increases this morning, as sampled \naround 40 knots just 2000 ft AGL on the KLOT VAD, it is no \nsurprise that shower coverage is gradually expanding over \nnorthern Illinois. PoPs were increased to around 30 to 40 \npercent chance to match the coverage being observed. This \nsouthwest to northeast oriented LLJ is expected to pivot and\nbecome slightly more easterly oriented through the morning and \nbe the driving force behind an increase in shower coverage. \nThere is modest effective shear present, but CAPE values and \nlapse rates are not providing confidence in overall coverage of \nthunder. That being said, there have already been small clusters\nof embedded thunderstorms develop early this morning. Most \nthunder is expected to be isolated, but there could be an uptick\nin thunder coverage right around and after daybreak. Current \nguidance is suggesting the LLJ will gradually diminish around \nmidday allowing the showers to eventually erode and sink\nsouthward as it falls apart. \n\nAn upper level ridge, the center of which is currently analyzed\nover Oklahoma and Texas, keeps northern Illinois and northwest \nIndiana along its northern periphery through midweek. There \nwill be plenty of dry times, but as embedded waves moves through\nthe pattern, there will be additional periods of showers and \nstorms. For example, RAP analysis is currently showing an upper \nlevel short wave from Rapid City, SD to around Dodge City, KS \njust behind ongoing storms in the Great Plains. There is an \nadditional short wave in western Wyoming and Colorado. Models \nare suggesting that that both these waves are expected to ride \nalong the top of the ridge and provide additional chances for \nprecipitation overnight tonight (the Plains wave) and again \novernight into Tuesday morning. If the ridge were to strengthen,\nit could force these waves (and subsequent shower/storm \nchances) farther north. Whether or not they maintain their \ncurrent trajectory or strength remains to be seen. For now, \nchances for showers and storms remain in the forecast through \nthe middle of the week.\n\nAs the ridge remains over the CONUS through midweek, so does \nsouthwesterly flow at the mid-levels. This wind direction will \naid in advecting in a warmer air mass to the western Great Lakes \nwith projected 850 mb temperatures over 20C, potentially pushing\n25C. Winds at the surface will also remain out of the south, \ngradually increasing dew points through the week back into the \n70s. Hotter temperatures in the upper 80s and lower 90s are \nexpected. This combination of hot temperatures and humid \nconditions are expected to increase heat indices into the mid to\nupper 90s by midweek, with the southwestern portion of the \nforecast area with a chance of exceeding 100. Chances for \nshower/storms through the weak lowers confidence in specific \ntemperatures values; therefore, there was no consideration at \nthis time for any heat hazard products (advisory/watch).\n\nModels are suggesting that the ridge could break down by the end\nof the work week. That will help the hottest of temperatures to\ncool off a bit, but it will return the upper level flow pattern \nto the northwest and thus bring with it additional chances for \nshowers/storms over the weekend.\n\nDK",
